    "html": "<h2>Blunders decide most games below master level</h2>\n<p>If you are stuck at the same rating, the cause is rarely your opening knowledge. It is the one move a game where you hang a piece, miss a one-move threat, walk into a tactic, or <a href=\"/blog/train-your-own-endgames\">let a won endgame slip</a>. At club level, most games are decided by a single blunder — one player hands the other a winning position, and the rest is formality.</p>\n<p>That is good news. It means you do not need to study everything. You need to stop making the handful of mistakes that actually cost you games.</p>\n<h2>Why “just calculate more carefully” does not work</h2>\n<p>Telling yourself to slow down and check for blunders works for about three moves, and then time pressure and habit take over. Blunder-avoidance is not willpower — it is pattern recognition. Strong players do not consciously check every move for hanging pieces; they have seen the pattern so many times that a loose piece or an open back rank simply looks wrong.</p>\n<p>Recognition like that is built by repeated exposure to the same patterns, not by reading about them once. That is the same principle behind the <a href=\"/blog/what-is-the-woodpecker-method\">Woodpecker Method</a>: see a pattern enough times, at spaced intervals, and it becomes automatic.</p>\n<h2>Train the positions you actually lose in</h2>\n<p>Generic puzzle sets teach you how to win — they are full of brilliant attacking shots. They do not teach you how to stop losing, because they are not drawn from your games and they do not target your blind spots.</p>\n<p>The fix is to train your own blunders:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Collect the exact positions where you went wrong. Run an engine over your recent games and pull out the moves where your <a href=\"/about\">winning chances</a> dropped sharply.</li>\n<li>Re-solve those positions, days apart, until the right move is obvious.</li>\n<li>Repeat on a widening schedule so the pattern moves into long-term memory.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>A simple weekly routine</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Import your last week of games from Chess.com or Lichess.</li>\n<li>Let an engine flag your blunders — a drop of roughly 15% in winning chances is a good threshold.</li>\n<li>Drill that set for ten minutes a day. First attempts are what count; retries do not.</li>\n<li>Each blunder you solve cleanly comes back on a longer interval; each one you miss comes back sooner.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Stop making the same mistake twice</h2>\n<p>The goal is not to never blunder again — everyone blunders. The goal is to stop repeating the same blunder. A queen left hanging on the same diagonal, the same back-rank pattern, the same overloaded defender: those are learnable, and once you have drilled them, they stop happening.</p>\n<p>PatternChess automates this loop. It pulls your games, finds your blunders with Stockfish, and schedules them with spaced repetition so you train the exact mistakes that are costing you rating. <a href=\"/login\">Try it on your own games</a> — it is free.</p>\n"
