    "html": "<h2>The idea in one sentence</h2>\n<p>The Woodpecker Method is a chess-training technique where you solve the same set of tactical positions over and over, each cycle faster than the last, until the patterns become instant recognition.</p>\n<p>It comes from the book of the same name by Axel Smith and Hans Tikkanen. Tikkanen used it to score three grandmaster norms in seven weeks — not by solving more puzzles, but by solving the same puzzles repeatedly.</p>\n<h2>Why repetition beats fresh puzzles</h2>\n<p>Most players grind an endless stream of new puzzles. It feels productive, but it trains recognition weakly: you see each pattern once and move on, and most of it is gone within a day, just as the forgetting curve predicts.</p>\n<p>Repetition flips this. The first pass through a set is slow and full of mistakes. The second is faster. By the fourth or fifth pass you are not calculating — you are recognizing. That recognition is exactly what you need over the board, where you do not have time to work everything out from scratch.</p>\n<h2>How the method works</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Build a set of tactical positions — a few hundred, all at a level you can mostly solve.</li>\n<li>Solve the entire set, marking the ones you get wrong.</li>\n<li>Wait, then solve the whole set again, faster.</li>\n<li>Repeat, shrinking the time between cycles, until the set is easy.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The shrinking interval is the engine of the method. Each pass should be quicker and more accurate than the last.</p>\n<h2>The problem with the classic version</h2>\n<p>The original method uses a fixed book of puzzles. They are well chosen, but they are not <em>your</em> puzzles. They do not target the specific patterns you keep missing, and assembling and tracking a personal set by hand is tedious.</p>\n<h2>Applying it to your own games</h2>\n<p>The strongest version of the Woodpecker Method drills the patterns you personally get wrong — the <a href=\"/blog/how-to-stop-blundering-in-chess\">blunders from your own games</a>. Instead of a generic book, your set becomes the exact positions where you lost ground, re-solved on a <a href=\"/about\">spaced-repetition</a> schedule.</p>\n<p>That is what PatternChess does: it finds your blunders with an engine and runs them through a Woodpecker-style loop automatically. <a href=\"/login\">Start with your own games</a> — it is free.</p>\n"
